## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical governance implementation using cross-account controls, automated remediation, and comprehensive cost visibility.

## Key Concepts
- AWS Organizations SCPs for tag enforcement across production accounts
- AWS Config custom rules for continuous tag compliance monitoring
- EventBridge for routing noncompliance events to remediation workflows
- Lambda functions for automated tag application and resource remediation
- AWS Budgets with per-account alerts for proactive cost governance

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ec2 = new AWS.EC2();

async function tagResource(arn, tags) {
  await ec2.createTags({
    Resources: [arn],
    Tags: Object.entries(tags).map(([k, v]) => ({ Key: k, Value: v }))
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to translate a cost/reliability requirement into concrete AWS components and thresholds for a real, beginner-friendly ECS setup.

## Key Concepts

- ECS Fargate Target Tracking scaling with min/max and target value
- ECS UpdateService to adjust desiredCount
- EventBridge scheduling and Step Functions workflow for pause/resume
- AWS Budgets alerts and CloudWatch alarms
- Practical testing and rollback plan

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: Update ECS Fargate service desired count via AWS SDK v3
const { ECSClient, UpdateServiceCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-ecs");
const client = new ECSClient({ region: "us-east-1" });
async function setCoreDesired(count){
  await client.send(new UpdateServiceCommand({
    cluster: "myCluster",
    service: "core-service",
    desiredCount: count
  }));
}
```

